//? Title: The AKS Primality Test: Deterministic, Polynomial and Slow
//? Subtitle: The polynomial Fermat identity, the choice of r, and what each step decides, with datamunge's algorithms::aks_trace
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
In 2002 Agrawal, Kayal and Saxena showed that primality can be decided
deterministically in time polynomial in \(\log n\). Their test starts from a
polynomial form of Fermat's little theorem, \((x+a)^n\equiv x^n+a \pmod n\),
which holds exactly when \(n\) is prime. To keep the degree small, the
identity is checked modulo \(x^r-1\) for a small \(r\) with
\(\operatorname{ord}_r(n)>\log_2^2 n\). We prove the identity in both
directions, sketch why the reduced checks still prove primality, and bound
\(r\) between \(\log_2^2 n\) and \(\log_2^5 n\). A step-by-step trace,
`algorithms::aks_trace`, shows which step decides each number. Up to 1000,
every composite is caught by a perfect-power or small-factor check, and
every prime from 37 on needs the full set of congruences. The congruences
reject a composite only when all its prime factors exceed \(r\), and then
at once. The chosen \(r\) is within a few per cent of \(\log_2^2 n\) up to 63
bits, far below the proven \(\log_2^5 n\). The cost grows as about
\(\log^{7} n\): a 20-bit prime takes about a second, where Miller-Rabin
takes under a microsecond.
)

\toc

> A polynomial Fermat test

**Lemma.** Let \(n\ge2\) and \(\gcd(a,n)=1\). Then \(n\) is prime if and
only if, in \((\mathbb Z/n)[x]\),

$$
(x+a)^n \equiv x^n + a \pmod n .
$$
\alttext(x plus a, to the n, is congruent to x to the n plus a, modulo n.)

*Proof.* The coefficient of \(x^k\) in \((x+a)^n\) is
\(\binom nk a^{n-k}\).

If \(n=p\) is prime, then \(p\mid\binom pk\) for \(0<k<p\), since \(p\)
divides \(p!\) but not \(k!\,(p-k)!\). By Fermat, \(a^p\equiv a\). So
\((x+a)^p\equiv x^p+a\).

If \(n\) is composite, let \(q\) be a prime factor with \(q^s\,\|\,n\).
Among the \(q\) consecutive numbers \(n, n-1, \dots, n-q+1\), only \(n\) is
divisible by \(q\). So \(\binom nq=n(n-1)\cdots(n-q+1)/q!\) is divisible by
exactly \(q^{s-1}\), and therefore not by \(q^s\). Since \(q\nmid a\), the
coefficient \(\binom nq a^{n-q}\) is non-zero modulo \(q^s\), and hence
modulo \(n\). \(\square\)

The test is exact but useless as it stands: \((x+a)^n\) has \(n+1\)
coefficients. AKS reduces modulo \(x^r-1\) as well, which folds exponents
modulo \(r\) and leaves \(r\) coefficients:

$$
(x+a)^n \equiv x^{n \bmod r} + a \pmod{x^r-1,\; n},\qquad a = 1,\dots,\ell,\quad \ell=\bigl\lfloor\sqrt{\varphi(r)}\,\log_2 n\bigr\rfloor .
$$
\alttext(x plus a, to the n, is congruent to x to the n mod r, plus a, modulo x to the r minus 1 and n, for a from 1 to ell, where ell is the floor of the square root of phi of r times log base 2 of n.)

A composite can satisfy one reduced congruence, so a single \(a\) is no
longer enough. The theorem is that \(\ell\) values are enough.

> Why the reduced checks suffice

**Theorem (AKS).** Suppose that:

- \(n\) is not a perfect power;
- \(n\) has no prime factor \(\le r\);
- \(\operatorname{ord}_r(n)>\log_2^2 n\);
- the reduced congruence holds for \(a=1,\dots,\ell\).

Then \(n\) is prime.

*Sketch.* Let \(p\) be a prime factor of \(n\). Call \(m\) *introspective*
for a polynomial \(f\) if \(f(x)^m\equiv f(x^m)\pmod{x^r-1,\,p}\).

- Both \(p\) (by Frobenius) and \(n\) (by hypothesis) are introspective
  for every \(f=\prod_a (x+a)^{e_a}\).
- Introspective numbers are closed under multiplication, so every
  \(n^ip^j\) is introspective.

Two groups give a contradiction:

- **The exponent group.** Let \(G\) be the subgroup of
  \((\mathbb Z/r)^\times\) generated by \(n\) and \(p\). Its size satisfies
  \(t=|G|\ge\operatorname{ord}_r(n)>\log_2^2 n\).
- **The polynomial group.** Let \(\mathcal G\) be the group generated by
  the \(x+a\) in the field \(\mathbb F_p[x]/(h)\), where \(h\) is an
  irreducible factor of the \(r\)-th cyclotomic polynomial.
- **Lower bound.** Distinct products of the \(x+a\) of degree below \(t\)
  stay distinct in \(\mathcal G\). So
  \(|\mathcal G|\ge\binom{t+\ell}{t-1}>n^{\sqrt t}\), using
  \(t>\log_2^2 n\).
- **Upper bound.** If \(n\) is not a power of \(p\), the numbers
  \(n^ip^j\) with \(0\le i,j\le\lfloor\sqrt t\rfloor\) give two exponents
  \(m\ne m'\) that are congruent modulo \(r\). Every element of
  \(\mathcal G\) is then a root of \(y^m-y^{m'}\). This forces
  \(|\mathcal G|\le n^{\sqrt t}\).

The bounds contradict each other, so \(n=p^k\). Since \(n\) is not a
perfect power, \(n=p\). \(\square\)

**How large is \(r\)?**

- Below: \(\operatorname{ord}_r(n)\le\varphi(r)<r\), so
  \(r>\log_2^2 n\).
- Above: every \(r\) whose order is at most \(B=\log_2^2 n\) divides
  \(\prod_{i\le B}(n^i-1)<2^{\log_2^5 n}\). The lcm of the first \(m\)
  integers is at least \(2^m\), so some \(r\le\lceil\log_2^5 n\rceil\)
  escapes.

Both \(r\) and \(\ell\le\sqrt r\log_2 n\) are therefore polynomial in
\(\log n\), and so is the test. `algorithms::aks_trace` runs it in five
steps:

1. reject perfect powers;
2. find \(r\) (`aks_r`);
3. reject any \(n\) with \(1<\gcd(a,n)<n\) for some \(a\le r\);
4. accept if \(n\le r\);
5. check the \(\ell\) congruences (`aks_congruence_holds`).

> The congruence at work

The first table expands \((x+1)^n\) with no reduction, so \(r>n\). The
second applies the reduced test with the \(r\) AKS would choose.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| n | (x+1)^n mod n, coefficients of x^0..x^n | middle terms vanish |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t n : {5, 6, 7, 9, 11, 15}) {
    const auto c = alg::aks_power(1, n, n + 1);  // r > n: no folding
    bool vanish = true;
    for (std::uint64_t k = 1; k < n; ++k) vanish = vanish && c[k] == 0;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", n, c, vanish ? "yes (prime)" : "no (composite)");
  }
  std::print("\n| n | r | (x+1)^n folded mod x^r-1: nonzero coefficients | congruence a = 1 |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t n : {97ULL, 341ULL, 561ULL, 1729ULL, 1009ULL * 1013}) {
    const auto r = alg::aks_r(n);
    const auto c = alg::aks_power(1, n, r);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r, std::ranges::count_if(c, [](auto v) { return v != 0; }),
               alg::aks_congruence_holds(n, r, 1) ? "holds" : "fails");
  }
}
```
