\image(figures/aks_r_growth.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The modulus \(r\) and the number of congruences \(\ell\) at a few bit lengths. The plot shows \(r\) for every bit length from 4 to 63, hugging the lower bound \(\log_2^2 n\), far below the proven upper bound)

The ratio \(r/\log_2^2n\) stays between 1.00 and 1.05 at every bit length
except 9, where \(n=509\) needs \(r=131\), 1.6 times the bound. The
number of congruences \(\ell\) is just below \(r\), because
\(\varphi(r)=r-1\) for prime \(r\) and \(\sqrt{r}\log_2 n\approx r\). So
in practice \(r\approx\ell\approx\log_2^2 n\).
\alttext(A plot of log10 r against bits from 4 to 63. Blue points rise smoothly just above a black curve for log2 n squared, from about 1.1 to about 3.6, with one point above the curve at 9 bits. A red curve for log2 n to the fifth rises far higher, to about 9.)

> Cost

One multiplication in \((\mathbb Z/n)[x]/(x^r-1)\) costs \(r^2\)
coefficient products with schoolbook arithmetic. Raising \(x+a\) to the
\(n\)-th power takes \(\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor\) squarings and \(\nu(n)-1\)
other multiplications, where \(\nu(n)\) is the number of 1 bits. A prime
needs all \(\ell\) congruences, so the coefficient products number about

$$
W(n) \approx \ell\,\bigl(\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor+\nu(n)\bigr)\,r^2 \;\approx\; \sqrt r\,\log_2 n\cdot 2\log_2 n\cdot r^2 \;=\; 2\,r^{5/2}\log_2^2 n .
$$
\alttext(W of n is about ell times log n plus nu of n, times r squared, which is about 2 r to the five halves times log squared n.)

With \(r\approx\log_2^2 n\), as observed, \(W\) grows as
\(\log^{7} n\). The worst-case analysis with \(r=O(\log^5 n)\) and fast
polynomial arithmetic gives the published
\(\tilde O(\log^{21/2} n)\). Lenstra and Pomerance later reduced this to
\(\tilde O(\log^6 n)\).

The block times the test on the largest prime below \(2^b\), for
\(b=8,\dots,20\). For comparison it also times Miller-Rabin, which the
library makes deterministic for 64-bit integers. It then compares the
measured time with \(W\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/aks_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> log_bits, log_aks, log_work, log_mr;
  std::print("| bits | n | r | ell | work W | AKS seconds | ns per product | Miller-Rabin ns |\n");
  std::print("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int b = 8; b <= 20; ++b) {
    std::uint64_t n = (std::uint64_t{1} << b) - 1;
    while (!alg::is_probable_prime(n)) --n;
    const auto plan = alg::aks_trace(n, false);
    const double work = static_cast<double>(plan.a_max) * (std::floor(std::log2(static_cast<double>(n))) +
                                                           std::popcount(n)) * static_cast<double>(plan.r * plan.r);
    auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const auto t = alg::aks_trace(n);
    const double seconds = std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    int primes = 0;
    for (int rep = 0; rep < 100000; ++rep) primes += alg::is_probable_prime(n);
    const double mr_ns = std::chrono::duration<double, std::nano>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count() / 100000;
    if (!t.is_prime || primes != 100000) std::print("unexpected verdict at {}\n", n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.2e} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {:.0f} |\n", b, n, t.r, t.a_max, work, seconds,
               1e9 * seconds / work, mr_ns);
    log_bits.push_back(std::log10(b));
    log_aks.push_back(std::log10(seconds));
    log_work.push_back(std::log10(work));
    log_mr.push_back(std::log10(mr_ns * 1e-9));
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame timing;
  timing.add_column("log_bits", log_bits);
  timing.add_column("log_seconds", log_aks);
  timing.add_column("log_work", log_work);
  const auto time_fit = dm::stats::LM(dm::formula("log_seconds ~ log_bits"), timing);
  const auto work_fit = dm::stats::LM(dm::formula("log_work ~ log_bits"), timing);
  std::print("\nSlope of log time on log bits: {:.2f}; slope of log W on log bits: {:.2f}\n",
             time_fit.coefficients()[1], work_fit.coefficients()[1]);
  // Draw W scaled to the measured time at 20 bits.
  const double shift = log_aks.back() - log_work.back();
  std::vector<double> scaled;
  for (double w : log_work) scaled.push_back(w + shift);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(log_bits, log_aks, "AKS", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.5)
      .lines(log_bits, scaled, "model W", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5)
      .points(log_bits, log_mr, "Miller-Rabin", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3.5)
      .y_limits(-8, 2)
      .title("Time to prove the largest prime below 2^b")
      .x_label("log10 bits b")
      .y_label("log10 seconds")
      .size(860, 500)
      .save_svg("build/aks_cost.svg");
}
```
