\image(figures/aks_cost.svg)
// result_end
\caption(AKS on the largest prime of 8 to 20 bits: the modulus, the number of congruences, the work model \(W\), the measured time, the time per coefficient product, and Miller-Rabin's time for the same number)
\alttext(A log-log plot of seconds against bits. Blue AKS points rise steeply along the blue work-model line, from about a millisecond at 8 bits to one second at 20 bits, with a bump at 9 bits. Red Miller-Rabin points stay nearly flat between 2 and 8 times 10 to the minus 7 seconds.)

Over 8 to 20 bits, log time rises against log bits with slope 7.3, close
to the 6.7 of the work model \(W\) and to the predicted 7. The time per
coefficient product drifts only from 0.22 to 0.40 ns, as the polynomials
outgrow the cache. The model even predicts the bump at 9 bits, where the
large \(r\) makes 509 slower to prove than 1021.

At 20 bits AKS takes about a second, against 0.8 microseconds for
Miller-Rabin, a factor of over a million. Extrapolating \(\log^7 n\), a
64-bit prime would take about an hour.

> Summary

- \((x+a)^n\equiv x^n+a\pmod n\) characterises primes exactly: the middle binomial coefficients vanish for primes, and for composites the coefficient of \(x^q\) survives, where \(q\) is a prime factor of \(n\). Reducing modulo \(x^r-1\) keeps the degree below \(r\). The AKS theorem shows that \(\lfloor\sqrt{\varphi(r)}\log_2 n\rfloor\) reduced checks then decide primality, provided \(\operatorname{ord}_r(n)>\log_2^2n\).
- Most composites never reach the polynomial step. Up to 1000, perfect powers and small factors catch every composite. A composite reaches the congruences only when all its prime factors exceed \(r\), and then fails at \(a=1\).
- The chosen \(r\) stays within 5% of \(\log_2^2 n\) for every bit length from 4 to 63 except one, far below the \(\log_2^5 n\) of the worst-case bound.
- The cost grows as about \(\log^7 n\), as the work model predicts: about a second at 20 bits, against under a microsecond for Miller-Rabin. AKS matters because it is unconditional and polynomial, not because it is practical.

> References

- M. Agrawal, N. Kayal, N. Saxena (2004). PRIMES is in P. *Annals of Mathematics* 160(2), 781\-\-793.
- H. W. Lenstra Jr., C. Pomerance (2019). Primality testing with Gaussian periods. *Journal of the European Mathematical Society* 21(4), 1229\-\-1269.
- A. Granville (2005). It is easy to determine whether a given integer is prime. *Bulletin of the AMS* 42(1), 3\-\-38.
